=== II. What It Is to Enter into Temptation

#smallcaps[Having] shown what temptation is, I come next to make plain what it is to #emph[enter] into temptation.

1\. This is not merely to #emph[be tempted]. It is impossible for us to be so free from temptation as not to be tempted at all. As long as Satan continues in his power and malice, and as long as the world and lust exist, we will be tempted. “Christ,” says one writer, “was made like us so that he might be tempted; and we are tempted so that we may be made like Christ.” Temptation in general takes in our whole warfare, just as our Savior calls the time of his ministry the time of his “temptations” (Luke 22:28). We have no promise that we will not be tempted at all. Nor are we to pray for absolute freedom from temptations, because we have no promise of being heard in that. The direction we have for our prayers is, “lead us not into temptation” (Matt 6:13). It is “entering into temptation” that we are to pray against. We may be tempted and yet not enter into temptation. So:

2\. Something more is meant by this expression than the #emph[ordinary work] of Satan and of our own lusts, which are sure to tempt us every day. There is something notable in this entering into temptation, something that is not the saints’ everyday work. It is something that befalls them in a special way with regard to being seduced to sin, on one account or another, by way of allurement or of fright.

3\. It is not #emph[to be conquered] by a temptation: to fall down under it, to commit the sin or evil we are tempted to, or to omit the duties it opposes. A man may “enter into temptation” and yet not fall under temptation. God can make a way for a man to escape. When he is in, God can break the snare, tread Satan down, and make the soul more than a conqueror, though it has entered into temptation. Christ #emph[entered] into it, but was not in the least #emph[defeated] by it. But:

4\. It is, as the apostle expresses it, ἐμπίπτειν, to “fall into temptation” (1 Tim 6:9), as a man falls into a pit or a deep place where there are traps and snares that entangle him. The man is not immediately killed and destroyed, but he is entangled and held fast; he does not know how to get free or be at liberty. It is expressed again to the same effect in 1 Cor 10:13: “No temptation has seized you#super[+]”; that is, to be seized by a temptation and entangled with it, held in its cords, finding no way to escape for the present. So Peter says, “the Lord knows how to rescue the godly from temptations” (2 Pet 2:9). They are entangled with them; God knows how to rescue them out of them. When we allow a temptation to enter into us, then we “enter into temptation.” While it knocks at the door we are at liberty. But when any temptation comes in and bargains with the heart, reasons with the mind, and entices and allures the affections, we “enter into temptation.” That is so whether it lasts a long time or a short one, and whether it does this without our sensing or perceiving it or the soul takes notice of it.

So, then, for our entering into temptation it is required:

(1.) That by some #emph[advantage], or on some occasion, Satan is more earnest than usual in his solicitations to sin, by frights or allurements, by persecutions or seductions, by himself or by others. Or else that some lust or corruption, through his urging and through the advantages of outward objects, stirs up a greater tumult within us than usual, provoking us, as in prosperity, or terrifying us, as in trouble. A special activity of the author and the principles of temptation is required for it.

(2.) That the heart is so far entangled with it as to be driven to #emph[dispute] and argue in its own defense, and yet is not able wholly to throw out or get rid of the poison and leaven that has been injected. Rather, if it is ever so little off its watch, it is caught by surprise in an entanglement not easily avoided. So the soul may cry, and pray, and cry again, and yet not be delivered; as Paul “pleaded with the Lord” three times for his temptation to depart, and did not prevail. The entanglement continues. And this usually happens in one of these two seasons:

\[1.\] When Satan, by God’s permission, for ends best known to God himself, has gained some #emph[special] advantage against the soul; as in the case of Peter. Satan sought to sift him, and he prevailed.

\[2.\] When a man’s lusts and corruptions meet with specially provoking #emph[objects] and occasions through the condition of life he is in and its circumstances; as it was with David. Of both of these I will speak later.

In this state of things a man has entered into temptation. And this is called the “hour of temptation” (Rev 3:10), the season in which it comes to a head. Uncovering what this is will throw further light on our present inquiry into what it is to “enter into temptation”; for when the hour of temptation has come upon us, we have entered into it. Every great and pressing temptation has its hour, a season in which it comes to a head, in which it is most vigorous, active, working, and prevailing. It may be a long time rising, and it may be long in pressing, more or less. But it has a season when, through the coming together of other circumstances, outward or inward, such as those mentioned, it has a dangerous hour; and then, for the most part, men enter into it. That is why the very temptation that at one time has little or no power over a man, so that he can despise it, scorn its promptings, and easily resist it, at another time carries him away completely before it. It has gained new strength and efficacy from other circumstances and events, or else the man is drained of strength and weakened. The hour has come, he has entered into it, and it prevails. David probably had temptations to adultery or murder before, in his younger days, as he had in the case of Nabal. But the hour of temptation had not come; the temptation had not gathered its advantages around it, and so he escaped until later. Let those who are exposed to temptations look out for it, and who is not exposed? They will have a season in which temptations will press harder, their reasonings will be more plausible, their pretexts more glorious, the hopes of recovery more in view, the opportunities wider and more open, and the doors of evil made more beautiful than ever before. Blessed is the man who is prepared for such a season; without that preparation there is no escape. This, as I said, is the first thing required for entering into temptation. If we stop here, we are safe.

Now that I have come to this, before I go down to other particulars I will show in general: 1st, how, or by what means, any temptation commonly reaches its #emph[hour]\; 2dly, how we may know when any temptation has come to its high #emph[noon] and is in its hour.

1st. It does the first in several ways:

(1st.) By long #emph[solicitations]. When it makes the mind often occupy itself with the evil it is urged to, this produces thoughts that make light of it. If temptation makes this progress, it is coming toward its hour. Perhaps when it first began to press on the soul, the soul was appalled at the ugly look of what it aimed at, and cried, “Am I a dog?” If this indignation is not heightened daily, and the soul, by dwelling with the evil, begins to grow familiar with it, as it were, and is not startled as it used to be, but is rather inclined to cry, “Is it not a little one?” then the temptation is coming toward its high noon. Lust has then enticed and entangled, and is ready to “conceive” (Jas 1:15). Of this I will speak more fully later, when we inquire how we may know whether we have entered into temptation or not. Our present inquiry is into the hour and power of temptation itself.

(2dly.) When it has prevailed over #emph[others], and the soul is not #emph[filled with dislike] and abhorrence of them and their ways, nor with pity and prayer for their deliverance. This proves an advantage to the temptation and raises it toward its height. When a temptation sets upon someone that has at the same time taken hold of and prevailed over many, it gains so great and so many advantages by this that it is surely growing toward its hour. Its prevailing over others is a means of giving it its hour against us. The falling away of Hymenaeus and Philetus is said to “undermine the faith of some” (2 Tim 2:17, 18).

(3dly.) By #emph[entwining] itself with many considerations that are perhaps not absolutely evil. So it was with the Galatians’ temptation to fall from the purity of the gospel: freedom from persecution, and union and agreement with the Jews. Things good in themselves were pleaded in it and gave life to the temptation itself. But I will not now dwell on the various advantages any temptation has to heighten and enlarge itself, to make itself prevailing and effectual, with the help it receives to this end from various circumstances, opportunities, plausible pleas and pretexts, necessities of doing what cannot be done without yielding to the temptation, and the like. For I must speak of some of them later.

2dly. As for the second, it may be known:

(1st.) By its restless #emph[urgency] and arguing. When a temptation is in its hour it is restless. It is the time of battle, and it gives the soul no rest. Satan sees his advantage and considers how his forces have come together. He knows that he must prevail now, or be without hope forever. Here are opportunities, here are advantages, here are plausible pleas and pretexts. Some ground has already been gained by earlier arguing. Here are excuses that make light of the evil, and hopes of pardon through later efforts, all ready at hand. If he can do nothing now, he must sit down defeated in his undertakings. So when he had got everything ready against Christ, he made it the “hour” of “darkness.” When a temptation uncovers “mille nocendi artes” \[a thousand arts of doing harm\], pressing within the house by imaginations and reasonings, and outside by solicitations, advantages, and opportunities, let the soul know that its hour has come. The glory of God, along with the soul’s own welfare, depends on how it behaves in this trial, as we will see in the particular cases that follow.

(2dly.) When it combines #emph[frights and allurements]. These two take in the whole forces of temptation. When both are brought together, temptation is in its hour. Both were present in David’s case in the murder of Uriah. There was the fear of Uriah’s revenge on his wife, and perhaps on David himself, and at least the fear of his sin being made public. And there was the allurement of his present enjoyment of the woman he lusted after. Men are at times carried into sin by love of it, and kept in it by fear of what will follow from it. But in any case where these two meet, where something allures us and something frightens us, and the reasonings that run between them are ready to entangle us, then it is the hour of temptation.

This, then, is what it is to “enter into temptation”; this is its “hour.” Of this I will say more as the discourse proceeds.

=== III. The Means of Prevention: Watch and Pray

III. Next there is the means of prevention prescribed by our Savior. There are two: 1\. “Watch”; 2. “Pray.”

1\. The first is a general expression, by no means to be limited to its native meaning of staying awake. To watch is the same as to be on our guard, to take heed, to consider all the ways and means by which an enemy may approach us. So the apostle uses it (1 Cor 16:13). This is what it is to “watch” in this business: to “stand firm in the faith,” as good soldiers, and to “be men of courage.” It is the same as προσέχειν, to “take heed” or look to ourselves, as our Savior often expresses the same thing; so too Rev 3:2. What this word presses on us is a universal care and diligence, exercising itself in and by all the ways and means God prescribes, over our hearts and ways, over the baits and methods of Satan, and over the occasions and advantages of sin in the world, so that we are not entangled.

2\. As for the second direction, prayer, I need not speak of it. The duty and what concerns it are known to all. I will only add that these two take in the whole endeavor of faith for the soul’s preservation from temptation.
